Returns a tensor containing the indices of all non-zero elements of input. Each row in the result contains the indices of a non-zero element in input. The result is sorted lexicographically, with the last index changing the fastest (C-style). If input has nnn dimensions, then the resulting indices tensor out is of size (zn)(z \times n)(zn), where zzz is the total number of non-zero elements in the input tensor. Note This function is similar to NumPys argwhere. When input is on CUDA, this function causes host-device synchronization. Parameters {input}  Example: >>> t = torch.tensor([1, 0, 1]) >>> torch.argwhere(t) tensor([[0], [2]]) >>> t = torch.tensor([[1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 1]]) >>> torch.argwhere(t) tensor([[0, 0], [0, 2], [1, 1], [1, 2]])
